  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 140 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nThursday through Monday:\n\nSynopsis: Upper troughing will linger over the western Great Lakes \nThursday into Thursday night, gradually becoming replaced by an area \nof upper ridging Friday night into Saturday. Beneath the upper \nheight falls, surface low pressure will migrate from the Lake \nSuperior vicinity toward Quebec Thursday into Friday. Progression of \nthe low will pull a secondary cold front through southern Wisconsin \nat some point Thursday afternoon or evening. Lift from the passing \nfront & lingering upper trough will support scattered rain chances \nThursday into perhaps Friday. Cooler conditions will prevail behind \nthe front Friday into Saturday. A clipper system will move into the \nUpper Mississippi Valley late Saturday night into Sunday, bringing \nchances for widely scattered rain showers. A precip-free forecast \nreturns by Monday.\n\nThursday through Friday: Scattered, hit & miss rain chances will \ncontinue, particularly during the day Thursday when ample amounts of \nsynoptic forcing will be available regionally. Anticipating greatest \npop-up shower coverage during the daytime hours Thursday, when \nsurface heating + frontal convergence will combine with cold mid-\nlevel temperatures to create very favorable conditions for \noverturning in the low-mid part of the column. NBM precip \nprobabilities do well in reflecting this peak potential, and have \nbeen maintained in the afternoon update. If upper troughing lingers \nlong enough, could see additional diurnal pop-ups near Lake Michigan \non Friday, though potential precip will be combating an absence of \nnear-surface forcing and drier air moving in from the north. Have \nmaintained slight chance (~20%) precip probabilities from the NBM \nacross the east for the time-being, but will be monitoring for \npossible removals as this part of the period draws closer. Not \nexpecting any hazards in Thursday-Friday showers.\n\nSunday: Passing clipper system will provide DVPA/forcing for rain, \nthough lift will need to overcome dry conditions in the low levels. \nCurrent slight chance (~20%) precip probabilities appear reasonable \ngiven limitations in set-up. Will be monitoring for changes later \nthis week. No hazards are expected in any precip.\n\nQuigley",
